WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.767
This exercise asks you to find notes that need follow-up.

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:10.397
The JSON document contains a Boolean flag, but the key name is missing from the starter condition.

00:00:10.397 --> 00:00:17.232
Look at the earlier JSONB example and identify which key stores that follow-up information.

00:00:17.232 --> 00:00:22.207
Replace the placeholder with the quoted key name, then run the query.

00:00:22.207 --> 00:00:28.532
Inspect the returned notes and confirm that each one represents a true follow-up flag.

00:00:28.532 --> 00:00:31.707
Notice that extracting JSON as text is different from comparing a Boolean.

00:00:31.707 --> 00:00:37.037
The example handles that conversion for you.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:40.657
Focus on the pattern: choose the JSON key, extract the value, cast it when necessary,

00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:44.042
then use the result in a condition.

00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:46.457
You do not need to memorize every JSON operator yet.

00:00:46.457 --> 00:00:51.637
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:51.637 --> 00:00:58.937
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:58.937 --> 00:01:00.802
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:00.802 --> 00:01:08.992
In a real setting such as an analyst exploring operational records before building a report,

00:01:08.992 --> 00:01:14.202
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:14.202 --> 00:01:17.087
before discussing syntax.

00:01:17.087 --> 00:01:23.288
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
